WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.566 --> 00:00:13.748
In about half an hour, I heard rustling in the thicket behind me.

2
00:00:14.628 --> 00:00:18.370
Bill staggered out of the bushes with the kid about eight feet behind him.

3
00:00:19.331 --> 00:00:23.793
The boy was stepping softly like a cat and had a broad grin on his face.

4
00:00:24.874 --> 00:00:28.956
Bill stopped, took off his hat, and wiped his face with a red handkerchief.

5
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.863
"Sam," Bill said, "you're going to think I betrayed you, but I couldn't help it.

6
00:00:35.784 --> 00:00:36.845
The boy is gone.

7
00:00:37.345 --> 00:00:38.286
I've sent him home.

8
00:00:38.506 --> 00:00:39.486
Everything is off.

9
00:00:40.127 --> 00:00:46.591
Old legends tell of men," Bill went on, "who suffered hardship and even death rather than give up

10
00:00:46.651 --> 00:00:47.832
in the face of adversity.

11
00:00:48.732 --> 00:00:51.694
But none of them was ever tortured as much as I've been."

12
00:00:51.714 --> 00:00:53.415
"What's the trouble?"

13
00:00:53.715 --> 00:00:54.216
I asked him.

14
00:00:55.284 --> 00:01:00.427
"He rode me," Bill said, "the ninety miles to the fort, not an inch less.

15
00:01:01.368 --> 00:01:04.569
Then when we rescued the settlers, I was given horse feed.

16
00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.612
Need I tell you that sand is not a substitute for oats?

17
00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:14.935
After that, I had to explain to him why there is nothing in a hole, how a road

18
00:01:14.995 --> 00:01:18.317
can run both ways, and what makes the grass green.

19
00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:22.700
I tell you, Sam, a man can only stand so much.

20
00:01:23.784 --> 00:01:26.685
I grabbed him by the collar and dragged him down the mountain.

21
00:01:27.546 --> 00:01:30.227
He kicked my legs black and blue all the way.

22
00:01:31.147 --> 00:01:35.189
Look! I've got three bites on my hand that should be seen by a doctor.

23
00:01:35.209 --> 00:01:38.051
"So he's gone," Bill continued.

24
00:01:38.071 --> 00:01:39.411
"Gone home.

25
00:01:39.851 --> 00:01:42.372
I showed him the road to Summit and shoved him toward it.

26
00:01:43.413 --> 00:01:48.855
I'm sorry that we're going to lose the ransom, but it was either that or Bill Driscoll ends

27
00:01:48.915 --> 00:01:49.896
up a crazy man."

28
00:01:51.084 --> 00:01:56.048
Bill was out of breath, but there was a look of overwhelming peace and growing contentment on his

29
00:01:56.108 --> 00:02:01.612
face. "Bill," I said, "there isn't any heart disease in your family, is there?"

30
00:02:01.632 --> 00:02:07.496
"No," he said, "nothing unusual except malaria and a few accidents.

31
00:02:07.716 --> 00:02:12.940
Why?" "Then turn around," I said, "and have a look behind you."

32
00:02:13.881 --> 00:02:17.524
Bill turned, saw the boy, and his face went white.

33
00:02:18.508 --> 00:02:22.549
He plopped down in the dirt and began to pluck at the grass and little sticks on the

34
00:02:22.589 --> 00:02:24.090
ground for no reason at all.

35
00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:26.910
I was afraid he'd lost his mind.

36
00:02:27.971 --> 00:02:32.332
And then I said, "My plan is to get this job over with immediately.

37
00:02:33.252 --> 00:02:37.714
We'll get the ransom and be on the road with the money by midnight if old Dorset agrees."

38
00:02:38.894 --> 00:02:40.494
Bill took a deep breath and nodded.

39
00:02:40.915 --> 00:02:42.915
He gave Red Chief a weak sort of smile.

40
00:02:42.935 --> 00:02:48.562
"As soon as I feel a little better, kid, I'll play General Custer and you can be Sitting

41
00:02:48.602 --> 00:02:50.383
Bull at the Battle of the Little Bighorn.

42
00:02:51.003 --> 00:02:51.644
I promise."

43
00:02:52.844 --> 00:02:56.207
I had a great plan for collecting the ransom so I wouldn't be caught.

44
00:02:57.027 --> 00:02:59.268
How proud kidnappers everywhere would be!

45
00:03:00.009 --> 00:03:05.552
The tree under which Dorset's answer was to be left—was next to the road, with big open fields

46
00:03:05.653 --> 00:03:06.453
on all sides.

47
00:03:07.294 --> 00:03:12.248
Naturally, if anyone might be watching for the kidnapper to come for the note, they'd be able to

48
00:03:12.348 --> 00:03:16.269
see him a long way off crossing the fields or walking in the road.

49
00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:18.330
But they wouldn't see me!

50
00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:24.312
At half-past eight, I was up in that tree, as well hidden as a tree toad, waiting for

51
00:03:24.352 --> 00:03:25.472
the messenger to arrive.

52
00:03:27.072 --> 00:03:32.154
Exactly on time, a boy rode up on a bicycle and found the cardboard box at the foot

53
00:03:32.174 --> 00:03:32.994
of the fence post.

54
00:03:33.814 --> 00:03:36.895
He slipped a folded piece of paper into it and pedaled away.

55
00:03:37.735 --> 00:03:40.016
I waited an hour until I was sure I was safe.

56
00:03:40.992 --> 00:03:45.893
Then I slid down the tree, got the note, crept along the fence until I hit the woods,

57
00:03:46.193 --> 00:03:48.454
and was back at the cave in another half an hour.

58
00:03:49.334 --> 00:03:51.635
I opened the note near the lantern and read it to Bill.

59
00:03:52.435 --> 00:03:59.357
Two Desperate Men Gentlemen: I received your letter today regarding the ransom for the return of my son.

60
00:04:00.317 --> 00:04:04.298
I think your demands are a little high, so I'll make you a counter-offer that I believe you

61
00:04:04.338 --> 00:04:05.039
will accept.

62
00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:11.204
You bring Johnny home and pay me two hundred and fifty dollars in cash, and I agree to

63
00:04:11.244 --> 00:04:12.425
take him off your hands.

64
00:04:13.565 --> 00:04:18.366
You'd better come at night, because the neighbors believe he is lost and gone for good.

65
00:04:19.406 --> 00:04:23.087
I won't be responsible for what they would do to you for bringing that boy back.

66
00:04:24.028 --> 00:04:32.310
Very respectfully, EBENEZER DORSET "For crying out loud," I said, "of all the nerve—" But I glanced at

67
00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:33.830
Bill, and I stopped.

68
00:04:34.371 --> 00:04:37.551
He had the most pleading look in his eyes I ever saw.

69
00:04:37.571 --> 00:04:42.446
"Sam," he said, "what's two hundred and fifty dollars, after all?

70
00:04:42.786 --> 00:04:43.586
We got the money.

71
00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:47.689
One more night of this kid will send me to the insane asylum.

72
00:04:48.669 --> 00:04:50.530
Mr. Dorset is giving us a bargain.

73
00:04:50.991 --> 00:04:53.272
You're not going to let this chance go, are you?"

74
00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:59.736
"To tell you the truth, Bill," I said, "this tender little lamb of a boy has gotten on

75
00:04:59.796 --> 00:05:00.756
my nerves too.

76
00:05:01.817 --> 00:05:04.759
We'll take him home, pay the ransom, and make our getaway."

77
00:05:05.419 --> 00:05:06.740
We took him back that night.

78
00:05:07.664 --> 00:05:08.965
"I don't want to go home!"

79
00:05:09.205 --> 00:05:10.866
the kid said when he saw his house.

80
00:05:11.827 --> 00:05:13.287
"Oh, yes you do," I told him.

81
00:05:13.648 --> 00:05:15.929
"Your father bought a silver rifle for you."

82
00:05:15.949 --> 00:05:18.590
"And a pair of moccasins," Bill added.

83
00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:19.791
"He did?"

84
00:05:20.271 --> 00:05:21.112
Red Chief asked.

85
00:05:22.052 --> 00:05:23.293
"Yep." Bill nodded.

86
00:05:23.313 --> 00:05:25.715
"And we're all going bear hunting tomorrow.

87
00:05:25.755 --> 00:05:26.355
You'll see."

88
00:05:27.155 --> 00:05:30.217
It was just twelve o'clock when we knocked on Ebenezer's front door.

89
00:05:30.918 --> 00:05:35.832
Just when I should have been taking the fifteen hundred dollars from the box under the tree, Bill

90
00:05:35.852 --> 00:05:38.935
was counting out two hundred and fifty dollars into Dorset's hand.

91
00:05:38.955 --> 00:05:41.096
"You're leaving me here?"

92
00:05:41.356 --> 00:05:43.057
the kid said when he figured things out.

93
00:05:43.077 --> 00:05:45.479
"NOOO! NOOO!"

94
00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.322
He howled like a wounded coyote and grabbed Bill's leg.

95
00:05:49.342 --> 00:05:56.287
"NOOO!" His father peeled him away gradually, as Red Chief continued to kick and scream.

96
00:05:56.307 --> 00:05:58.388
"How long can you hold him?"

97
00:05:58.648 --> 00:05:59.189
asked Bill.

98
00:05:59.209 --> 00:06:05.225
"I'm not as strong as I used to be," said Old Dorset, "but I think I can promise

99
00:06:05.265 --> 00:06:06.305
you ten minutes."

100
00:06:07.225 --> 00:06:08.746
"That's enough," said Bill.

101
00:06:08.766 --> 00:06:12.327
"In ten minutes, I'll be crossing the Canadian border."

102
00:06:13.187 --> 00:06:14.928
Bill took off running through the darkness.

103
00:06:15.728 --> 00:06:20.329
And even though he was very fat and I'm a very good runner, he was a good mile

104
00:06:20.369 --> 00:06:22.950
and a half out of Summit before I could catch up with him.
